# PART 1 — CORE DEVELOPMENT RULES

1. Build a new, clean, professional project. Do not copy the old implementation blindly.
2. Do not carry over insecure or demo-only functionality from the previous website.
3. Build the entire product: frontend, backend, database, authentication, admin dashboard, reader, commerce, and security.
4. Do not use localStorage as the authoritative database for users, orders, payments, purchases, pricing, or admin settings.
5. All important business operations must be performed and verified on the server.
6. Never fake a successful payment, purchase, authentication, or book unlock.
7. Never expose private API keys, service-role keys, payment secrets, or privileged credentials in frontend code.
8. Use clean architecture, reusable components, typed interfaces, validation, and maintainable code.
9. Do not leave important features as buttons that do nothing.
10. Do not claim that a feature is complete until it has actually been implemented and tested.
11. Do not sacrifice security to make a feature appear functional.
12. Do not stop after generating a roadmap or a design. Build the actual working application.

Before implementation, inspect the working directory and available development environment. If a project already exists in the target directory, inspect it before changing anything. Do not overwrite unrelated user files.

