# PART 10 — REAL PAYMENT ARCHITECTURE

Payment is a critical commercial feature.

Do not implement a fake gateway.

Build a provider-independent payment architecture that can support the payment providers selected by the business.

### Requirements

- Payment provider adapter interface.
- Secure server-side payment initiation.
- Unique order reference.
- Unique transaction reference.
- Payment status tracking.
- Provider response validation.
- Trusted webhook endpoint.
- Webhook signature verification.
- Idempotent webhook processing.
- Duplicate callback protection.
- Payment reconciliation support.
- Failed payment handling.
- Cancelled payment handling.
- Refund record support.
- Transaction history.

### Critical payment rule

A payment is successful ONLY when the backend has verified it through a trusted provider response or verified webhook.

Never:
- Mark a payment successful because the customer returns to a success URL.
- Trust a browser-submitted transaction status.
- Unlock a book before verified payment.
- Expose provider secret keys in the frontend.
- Create a fake transaction to make the checkout look functional.

### Provider configuration

Create an architecture allowing authorized administrators to configure supported payment methods.

Do not invent provider names, credentials, endpoints, or integration status.

If real provider credentials and official integration documentation are not available, implement the secure adapter interface and configuration structure, mark that provider as not connected, and report exactly what is required to complete the integration.

Do not pretend a real payment provider is operational when it is not.

### Manual payment methods

If manual payment methods are enabled by the administrator, support:
- Payment instructions.
- Customer-submitted transaction reference.
- Pending verification.
- Admin verification or rejection.
- Audit trail.

Manual payment must not automatically become verified just because a customer submits a reference.

